WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.435
C does not evaluate every subexpression in the intuitive left-to-right order developers may assume.

2
00:00:06.435 --> 00:00:11.973
Sequencing rules define when side effects are complete relative to other evaluations.

3
00:00:11.973 --> 00:00:21.476
If an expression modifies an object and reads or modifies it again without defined sequencing, the program can leave the defined rules of the language.

4
00:00:21.476 --> 00:00:26.190
Compact expressions can be appealing, but they are often harder to review.

5
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:34.571
A statement that increments, indexes, assigns, calls a function, and reads a value in one expression may hide ordering assumptions.

6
00:00:34.571 --> 00:00:40.483
Security-sensitive C code benefits from clear statements where each side effect is visible.

7
00:00:40.483 --> 00:00:51.707
Undefined behavior can also come from out-of-bounds access, invalid pointer use, signed overflow, invalid shifts, uninitialized reads, lifetime violations, and data races.

8
00:00:51.707 --> 00:00:58.292
The common theme is that the implementation is not required to preserve the result a developer expected.

9
00:00:58.292 --> 00:01:04.054
Optimizers can assume undefined behavior does not happen and transform code accordingly.

10
00:01:04.054 --> 00:01:10.265
That means a program may appear stable with one compiler flag and behave differently with another.

11
00:01:10.265 --> 00:01:17.673
The correct response is not to fight the optimizer, but to write code whose assumptions are inside the language rules.

12
00:01:17.673 --> 00:01:28.449
Defensive review favors simple sequencing, checked arithmetic, clear lifetime boundaries, explicit initialization, and small expressions with one obvious purpose.

13
00:01:28.449 --> 00:01:33.762
Readability is not just style; it is a way to make memory-model assumptions visible.
